 "repeats": 50,
 "method": "Both backends warmed per case; repeats rounds with the run order flipping each round (N,T then T,N); outputs compared on every run. kernel_ms/total_ms are each driver's own perf_counter + synchronize measurements, launch overhead included. speedup = numba_ms / triton_ms (>1: Triton faster). kernel_ms is primary; total_ms also compares the allocators (CuPy pool vs Numba cuMemAlloc). Rows with comparable=false are not like-for-like and stay out of the summary's averages.",
 "meta": {
  "source_benchmark": "experiments/scan_multi_blob/scan_only.py main_small_example (timeit around process_image_small_example)",
  "quick": false,
  "caps": [],
  "notes": [
   "Grid is 1 block of 100 threads (Numba [1, 100]) = 1 program of 128 lanes, 100 active (Triton (1,), num_warps 4) on both sides.",
   "Numba's stop flag and clock are shared memory; the twin's are a global scratch allocated outside the timed brackets. One clock atomic per lane per step on both sides (shared-memory atomics in Numba, L2 atomics in Triton).",
   "One block on a 100x100 image. small_example stops after its first step (about 10 us on the GPU on both sides), so its kernel_ms is mostly launch + synchronize overhead; early_stop and simple_scan run 60-150 us on the GPU. info.device_us is the GPU-only time (CUDA events, queue kept busy).",
   "Outputs are nondeterministic by construction; same() checks only the schedule-free parts (see compare.py)."
  ]
 },
